    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps account for only 2.7% of volume and 2 sets, which is the lowest of any muscle group. While biceps are not critical for the bench press goal, persistently low volume may limit elbow stability and overall arm development balance."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Prioritize back volume next period by adding at least 2 to 3 sets of horizontal or vertical pulling movements such as rows or pull-downs. The back index dropped 5 points this period and needs active recovery to support your pressing mechanics and long-term shoulder health.",
    "Maintain the current chest frequency and loading since the strength index rose 6 points this week. Focus on quality execution and incremental load increases on your main bench press sets to keep riding this momentum toward 100 kg.",
    "Check your recovery inputs around back training specifically, including sleep and overall fatigue levels on the days you train pulling movements. A sudden 5-point drop often reflects a recovery or execution issue rather than a true loss of strength.",
    "Consider adding one extra set for biceps over the coming period, bringing total biceps sets to 3 or 4. This modest increase supports elbow tendon health and maintains better pulling-to-pushing balance as your bench press loads continue to climb."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Chest strength index jumped 6 points to 112 and triceps gained 3 points, but back dropped 5 points to 96, triggering a regressing status; key focus for next period is recovering back volume and strength while maintaining chest momentum toward the 100 kg bench press goal."
